# fixedbugs, defect 2224, panel 230's R4, 2026-10-26: `exit(code:)` took an
# `i64` and ended the program with its low eight bits, so `exit(code: 256)`
# told the shell the program succeeded, and said nothing. A status is a byte
# now, `exit(code: u8)`: a literal outside 0 to 255 is refused as a literal
# that does not fit, and a computed `i64` until it is converted, `to_u8(n)`
# answering whether it fits.
#
# Amended 2026-10-29, task 2250 (panel 230's fallback, its condition met by
# decision 2241): `exit` takes an `i64` again. A literal outside 0 to 255 is
# still refused where it is written, told as a status rather than as a width,
# and a computed code is accepted, stopping the program at its call where it
# falls outside them (`run/abort-exit-code-outside-a-byte`), so the last line
# below, refused until then, is a program's.

function main()
    n = 3

    if n > 2
        exit(code: 256)  #~ int_out_of_range

    if n > 1
        exit(code: -1)  #~ int_out_of_range
    exit(code: n)
